<IndustryPage {...pageData}>
  <p>Creative agencies have a software problem that nobody talks about. Your work is visual, iterative, and client-driven - but most project management tools were built for software development or construction. So you end up with a PM tool that does not understand design rounds, a time tracker that nobody uses because it takes too long, and a client feedback process that lives in email threads nobody can find three months later.</p>

  <p>The typical agency tech stack is three or four SaaS tools loosely held together with hope. Monday.com for project tracking. Harvest for time tracking. A separate invoicing tool. Google Drive for assets. Nothing talks to anything else. Project profitability is a spreadsheet someone updates when they have time, which means never. Client approvals are buried in email, and scope creep goes unnoticed until the project is two weeks overdue and losing money.</p>

  <p>Bespoke software solves this by starting with your agency's actual workflow. Your project management tool understands that a creative project has briefs, design rounds, client feedback, revisions, and final delivery - not just a list of tasks. Your time tracker is integrated into the project workflow, so tracking time is a one-click action, not a separate tool to remember at the end of the day. Your client portal is branded with your identity and tracks every comment, revision, and approval - creating an auditable record of what was agreed and when.</p>

  <p>The biggest impact is usually on profitability. Generic PM tools tell you a project is in progress. Bespoke software tells you a project has consumed 40 hours against a 60-hour budget, that the client has requested two extra revision rounds, and that at the current rate you will lose £2,000 on it. That visibility - real-time, not retrospective - is the difference between profitable agencies and busy agencies.</p>

  <p>Integration with accounting is where the real efficiency lives. Time tracked against projects automatically generates draft invoices. Approved work flows into Xero or QuickBooks without manual entry. Overdue invoices trigger automated chase emails. Month-end reporting takes minutes, not days. One system, from brief to invoice, with no manual data transfer in between.</p>

  <p>A UK digital agency with 12 staff was using four separate tools - Monday.com, Harvest, Xero, and Google Drive - costing £8,400 a year in subscriptions. Project profitability was calculated manually in spreadsheets, usually a month after projects finished. We built a single platform that handled project management, time tracking, client approvals, asset management, and Xero integration. Build cost: £28,000. The agency identified two consistently unprofitable client retainers within the first month of using the real-time profitability dashboard, saving £24,000 a year. They own the software outright - no more subscription fees.</p>

  <p>If your agency is spending more than £5,000 a year on software that does not quite fit, <a href="/services">explore our services</a> or <a href="/contact">get in touch</a> to discuss whether bespoke software makes sense for your workflow.</p>
